## The Process to File a **CA Lemon Law

Claim

- Contact the manufacturer and report your vehicle’s defect as soon as possible. The manufacturer’s representative will direct you to a nearby certified repair facility.

- Gather all documentation for the repairs:

- Problem(s) with the vehicle

- Date of repairs

- Time spent in the repair facility

- Invoices

- Receipts

- If the repairs are unsuccessful, your next move is to contact a CA lemon law attorney to begin filing a claim.

## CA Lemon Law** and Used Vehicles

California lemon law extends benefits for used vehicles - but the criteria is slightly different than new vehicles. Here’s what you need to know when filing a claim for a used vehicle:

## Dealer Warranties

Many used vehicles sold at car dealerships come with dealer warranties. These are commonly good for 30 days after purchase or before 1,000 miles accrue on the odometer.

If you can prove your used vehicle meets the qualifications of California lemon law while the dealer warranty was valid, you are eligible to file a claim.

“As Is” Agreements

“As is” agreements typically mean there is no warranty - and no coverage under CA lemon law rights. The consumer takes complete responsibility for any issues after purchase. For used vehicles sold at dealerships, “as is” agreements must be displayed on the buyer’s guide.

If the buyer’s guide does not clarify a dealer warranty or “as is” agreement, the vehicle may have an [implied warranty of merchantability](https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/civil-code/civ-sect-1791-1/). This is an unspoken agreement that typically covers the essential components of the vehicle. You will need a skilled Beaumont CA lemon law lawyer to pursue benefits under an implied warranty.

